<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zwentibold</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 870–<a href="/years/900">900</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Zwentibold of Lotharingia · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-dynasty">Carolingian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zwentibold was the illegitimate son of the East Frankish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnulf-of-carinthia">Arnulf of Carinthia</a>, who made him king of Lotharingia in 895. His rule provoked the Lotharingian nobility, led by Reginar, and after Arnulf's death he was killed in battle on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> on 13 August 900. Lotharingia then passed to his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-child">Louis the Child</a>, and Zwentibold was later venerated locally as a saint.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zwentibold.jpg" alt="Zwentibold" width="244" height="251" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ekkehard von Aura · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zwentibold-king.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zwentibold, a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-dynasty">Carolingian dynasty</a>, was born about 870 during the long reign of his great-grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a>. He was the first-born but illegitimate son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnulf-of-carinthia">Arnulf of Carinthia</a> and his concubine Vinburga. His name is a Frankish form of Svatopluk, the ruler of Great Moravia, who was his godfather.</p><p>Arnulf, himself an illegitimate son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carloman-of-bavaria">Carloman of Bavaria</a>, succeeded King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-fat">Charles the Fat</a> as king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a> in 887. Zwentibold was at first marked out as his heir, and when he came of age he intervened in the struggle for the West Frankish throne between Count Odo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-simple">Charles the Simple</a>, who began to cooperate against him when it became clear that he intended to become king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When Arnulf's wife Ota gave birth to a legitimate son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-child">Louis the Child</a>, Zwentibold received the Lotharingian royal title in compensation; it had last been held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothair II</a>. Lotharingia, the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Francia</a>, had fallen to the East Frankish realm under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-meerssen">Treaty of Meerssen</a> of 870 and the Treaty of Ribemont of 880. In the summer of 893 Arnulf, answering pleas from Pope Formosus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berengar-i-of-italy">Berengar of Friuli</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-iii-of-spoleto">Wido of Spoleto</a>, sent Zwentibold over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brenner-pass">Brenner Pass</a> with an army. With Berengar he besieged Pavia without success.</p><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liutprand-of-cremona">Liutprand of Cremona</a>, Zwentibold accepted money from Wido to leave. His retreat was seen as a failure, and Arnulf led a stronger army to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and took Pavia a few months later. Zwentibold's rule in Lotharingia was enforced by his father, supported by Archbishop Herman I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> and Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radbod-of-trier">Ratbod of Trier</a>, against the resistance of the local nobility. The account holds that he helped the common people too much and came to be hated. He was fighting unruly nobles when Arnulf died in 899 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-child">Louis the Child</a> became king at the age of six.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Zwentibold tried to use his half-brother's youth to make Lotharingia fully independent. Having lost his father's backing, he saw the whole nobility support Louis, who was asked to intervene. In 900 Count Reginar I of Hainault and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odoacer">Odoacer</a> rose against him and killed him in battle on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">River Meuse</a>, near present-day Susteren, on 13 August. He is buried at Susteren Abbey. Louis the Child then ruled Lotharingia, but his realm disintegrated, and from 903 the Lahngau count Gebhard, of the Conradine dynasty, administered Zwentibold's former kingdom with the title Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>. Zwentibold had three daughters, Benedetta, Cecilia and Relenda, named in the Gesta episcoporum Leodensium, and a son, Otto de Lorraine, who died in 949.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zwentibold” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Zwentibold</b>, king of Lotharingia since 895, was killed in battle on the Meuse near present-day Susteren by nobles who had risen against him under Reginar Longneck.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-13">13 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 898 King <b>Zwentibold</b> granted Archbishop Ratbod immunity and comital rights over the city, the basis of the archbishops' temporal power.</blockquote><a href="/state/electorate-of-trier">Electorate of Trier</a></li><li><blockquote>Arnulf of Carinthia made his son <b>Zwentibold</b> king in 895, but he was killed in 900, and the land became the Duchy of Lotharingia.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lotharingia">Kingdom of Lotharingia</a></li></ul></section>
